## §1.2.3: Explicit Construction of Pullbacks in $\Ctx$ {.smaller}

<div class="stratum-badge badge-a">STRATUM A: PROPOSITION 1.9 / 1.13 (OVERLAP GEOMETRY)</div>

:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="52%"}
Given morphisms $\alpha \colon V_1 \to U$ and $\beta \colon V_2 \to U$, how do two observers compare findings on their shared overlap?

::: {.thm-box}
**Proposition 1.9 / 1.13: Categorical Pullback $W \coloneqq V_1 \times_U V_2$**

1. **Componentwise Fiber Products in $\Ban$ (with $\ell^\infty$ direct-sum norm):**
$$
F^W \coloneqq F^{V_1} \times_{F^U} F^{V_2} = \big\{ (x_1, x_2) \in F^{V_1} \oplus_\infty F^{V_2} \;\big|\; \alpha_F(x_1) = \beta_F(x_2) \big\}
$$
for $F \in \{D, P, C\}$. Using $\oplus_\infty$ guarantees that canonical projections $\pi_{1F}, \pi_{2F}$ have operator norm $\le 1$.

2. **Induced Contractive Coordination $K^W \colon D^W \otpi P^W \to C^W$:**
$$
\|K^W(u)\|_{C^W} = \max\big(\|K_1(u)\|_{C^{V_1}}, \|K_2(u)\|_{C^{V_2}}\big) \le \max\big(\|K^{V_1}\|_{\op}, \|K^{V_2}\|_{\op}\big)\|u\| \le 1
$$

3. **Diagonal Isometric Ciphers:** $z^W(p_1, p_2) \coloneqq (z^{V_1}(p_1), z^{V_2}(p_2)) \in \Isom(P^W)$.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="48%"}

<div class="video-container">
  <video autoplay data-autoplay loop muted playsinline src="media/Act7_3D_Pullback.mp4" style="max-height:330px;"></video>
  <div class="video-caption">Manim Act VII (Fig. 1.4): 3D ℓ^∞ Unit Cube &amp; Pullback Equalizer Slice W = V₁ ×_U V₂</div>
</div>

::: {.intuition-box .fragment}
**Next Step (§1.3–1.5):** Equipping $\CtxPos$ with contractive split-section coverings $\sigma_F$ ($\rho_F \circ \sigma_F = \operatorname{id}_{F^U}$) generates the **subcanonical Grothendieck topology $\Jsyn$** and the **Holoform Equalizer $\Hol(\mathcal{U}, \mathbf{P})$**.
:::

:::

::::

::: {.notes}
To define a Grothendieck topology on Ctx_adm^sk, we first need pullbacks so that covering families can be restricted along arbitrary context morphisms. By equipping the direct sum with the l-infinity norm, the equalizer subspace W = V_1 x_U V_2 inherits contractive projections and diagonal isometric ciphers.
:::

